### Page 6 of 15

```text
UDC-22-00425
January 3, 2023
The Future Land Use Designation for this property and most surrounding properties is Urban
Residential. According to comprehensive plan there are many optimal City zones for the subject
, including NR-3 Neighborhood Residential  High Density.

Policies in PlanCheyenne also encourage a variety of residential development patterns, and the
NR-3 zone allows a wide variety of residential uses.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 1.

2) The application is compatible with nearby property, specifically considering existing zoning,
the Comprehensive Plan, and any other official plans and policies created under the guidance
of that plan for these areas; (UDC 2.2.1.d.2)

Nearby properties to the west, south, and southeast within City limits are zoned MR and
developed with single-family homes. To the northeast, property within City limits is zoned NR-3
and developed with apartments. Property to the north and some land to the east are outside
City limits and developed with rural residential uses. Property immediately to the south is
vacant land in the County. The comprehensive plan anticipates urban residential development
around the subject property.

While allowing a higher density than most of the surrounding zones, the NR-3 zone would allow
for a variety of uses and is compatible with the mix of uses already found in the neighborhood.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 2.

3) The suitability of the subject property for development according to the standards and uses of
the existing zoning district, compared to the suitability of the subject property for
development according to the standards and uses of the proposed zoning district (UDC
2.2.1.d.3)

The current zoning is a County zone and a City zone is required to develop within City limits. The
City zone most analogous to the current County A-1 zone is the City AG zone. The AG zone is
intended for agricultural uses on large lots, but can also be used as a holding zone. The NR-3
zone does not require any buffers, and residential uses would be much more compatible with
neighboring property.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 3.

4) The ability of the subject property and surrounding vicinity to be developed according to the
Subdivision Standards in Article 4 considering the proposed zoning (UDC 2.2.1.d.4)

Due to the constraints presented by previous development patterns, it is unlikely this property
can meet the block standards of Article 4 for any proposed development; however, there is
potential to make a connection through the property and stub out to the south. The existing
block is about 180% oversize, and exceptions for various purposes are written into article four.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 4.
```

### Page 7 of 15

```text
UDC-22-00425
January 3, 2023
5)
The ability of the application to support the Intent and Applicability statements of the
proposed zoning district and achieve the Design Objectives of any specific standards. (UDC
2.2.1.d.5)

The NR-3 zone is intended to develop with a variety of residential uses, typically in areas with a
compact block pattern less than a ¼ mile from commercial development. This area is largely
already developed with a variety of residential uses, but the block pattern is not particularly
compact and there are few developed commercial or employment uses within a ¼ mile.
Although there are limited opportunities to have commercial uses within walking distance, the
land is near a major park and major traffic corridors. Higher intensity uses are appropriate near
major transportation corridors.

The proposed zone change for the subject property largely complies with review criterion 5.

6) The ability of all potential future development to meet all applicable standards for
development in the proposed zoning district, including other development options if any
specific development proposed in association with the application is not executed after
approval. (UDC 2.2.1.d.6)

The specific development proposed in concert with this rezoning is apartments. Should the
proposed development not be executed after approval, there are a number of residential uses
which would be eligible to be established at this location.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 6.

7) The review and recommendations of other reviewing bodies. (UDC 2.2.1.d.7)

No reviewing agencies provided recommendations regarding the proposed zone change.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 7.

8) Whether the application will harm the public health, safety, or general welfare. (UDC
2.2.1.d.8)

City staff is satisfied that this proposed zone change would not adversely affect the public
health, safety, or general welfare.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 8.

CONFORMANCE WITH APPLICABLE PLANS:

The project is located in an area covered by the following plans:

PlanCheyenne
Conformance with PlanCheyenne is covered by review criterion 1.

STAFF REVIEW:

Through the review process, no review comments were received.
```

### Page 8 of 15

```text
UDC-22-00425
January 3, 2023
CONCLUSION:

Staff is satisfied that the application meets the review criteria set forth in UDC 2.2.1.(d). The NR-3 zone
is appropriate based on the comprehensive plan and context of the property.

ALTERNATIVES:

1. Recommend approval of the zoning amendment as submitted by the applicant (Staff
Recommendation).
2. Recommend approval of the zoning amendment subject to conditions or modifications.
3. Recommend denial of the zoning amendment based on findings of denial. The Planning
Commission must conclude that the project does not meet all the review criteria. As part of the
motion, findings for denial must be stated.
4. Postpone the zoning amendment until issues identified during the meeting can be resolved.

General Information Regarding Alternatives:

If
Commission shall cite specific reasons (based on the review criteria) for approval or denial.

STAFF RECOMMENDATION:

Staff recommends approval of the rezoning of the property from County A-1 Agricultural and Rural
Residential to NR-3 Neighborhood Residential  High Density.

ATTACHMENTS:
1. Current Zoning Map
2. Future Land Use Map
3. Master Application
4. Applicant Provided Zone Change Map
